## Claim
Text: "UK Employees unable to work in tier 3 areas will get a combination of Job Support Scheme and Universal Credit, which will mean they get 80% of their wages."
Claim author: Boris Johnson
Claim date: October 21, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### Was there a UK government policy announced in October 2020 that provided financial support to employees in Tier 3 areas who were unable to work due to COVID-19 restrictions?
Answer: Yes, the UK government announced the Job Support Scheme in October 2020 to provide financial support to employees in Tier 3 areas unable to work due to COVID-19 restrictions, replacing the furlough scheme in November.

Source URL: https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/54633147

### What were the specific eligibility criteria for an employee to qualify for support under the Job Support Scheme (JSS) as announced in October 2020?
Answer: To qualify for the Job Support Scheme (JSS) as announced in October 2020, employees "must have been on an employer’s Pay As You Earn (PAYE) payroll system on or before 23 September 2020" and "must also work at least 33 percent of their usual hours."

Source URL: https://ogletreedeakins.co.uk/insights-resources/blog-posts/uk-government-introduces-new-job-support-scheme-to-protect-jobs-still-affected-by-the-covid-19-crisis/

### Did the UK government's official guidance state that employees in Tier 3 areas who were unable to work could receive a combination of the Job Support Scheme and Universal Credit?
Answer: Yes, the UK government's official guidance did state that employees in Tier 3 areas unable to work could receive a combination of the Job Support Scheme and Universal Credit, but with the specific condition that this would result in at least 80% of normal income only for those on low incomes receiving the full UC entitlement. The Department for Work and Pensions confirmed: "Those on low incomes getting the full entitlement [of Universal Credit] will receive at least 80% of their normal income."

Source URL: https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/54633147

### How was the payment from the Job Support Scheme (JSS) calculated for employees whose workplace was closed or who had no work?
Answer: The payment from the Job Support Scheme (JSS) for employees of businesses required to close due to coronavirus restrictions was calculated as the government paying "two thirds of each employees’ salary (or 67%), up to a maximum of £2,100 a month." Employers were not required to contribute to wages, only to cover National Insurance contributions and pension contributions.

Source URL: https://www.gov.uk/government/news/job-support-scheme-expanded-to-firms-required-to-close-due-to-covid-restrictions

### How was the payment from Universal Credit (UC) calculated for an employee with no other income in October 2020?
Answer: For an employee with no other income in October 2020, the Universal Credit payment was calculated by first determining their household’s maximum entitlement based on circumstances (like a standard allowance and any additional elements for children or housing), and then applying any relevant deductions (such as for sanctions or debt). Since they had no other income like earnings or savings, their payment would primarily reflect this maximum amount, minus any applicable reductions.

Source URL: https://www.understandinguniversalcredit.gov.uk/new-to-universal-credit/how-much-youll-get/

### When combining the payments from the Job Support Scheme and Universal Credit, did the UK government's policy or guidance state that the total would equal 80% of the employee's previous wages?
Answer: The UK government's official guidance did not state that all workers would receive a total of 80% of their previous wages through the combination. While Prime Minister Boris Johnson made this claim, the Department for Work and Pensions clarified that the 80% figure applied only to "those on low incomes getting the full entitlement" of Universal Credit, not as a universal guarantee.

Source URL: https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/54633147

### Was the 80% of wages figure a guaranteed minimum, an average estimate, or a maximum cap under this combined support system?
Answer: The 80% figure was **neither a guaranteed minimum, a universal average, nor a formal cap**, but rather an ideal outcome that applied only to some low-income workers eligible for full Universal Credit. For others, the combined support could be lower—for example, one cited case yielded only 73% of usual income.

Source URL: https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/54633147

### Were there any earnings caps or limits applied to the 80% of wages support mentioned in the claim?
Answer: Yes, the government's contribution under the Job Support Scheme was capped at £697.92 per month, which could result in support falling below the claimed 80% for higher-earning employees.

Source URL: https://ogletreedeakins.co.uk/insights-resources/blog-posts/uk-government-introduces-new-job-support-scheme-to-protect-jobs-still-affected-by-the-covid-19-crisis/

### Did the policy apply to all employees in Tier 3 areas unable to work, or were there sector-specific exclusions or variations?
Answer: No, the policy did not apply uniformly to all employees in Tier 3 areas unable to work; eligibility for the claimed 80% combination of support depended on individual financial circumstances such as income, savings, and family status, not on sector-specific exclusions. As the BBC report explains, "Whether a worker gets Universal Credit on top of the job support scheme depends on a number of factors such as the level of their income, whether they have savings and whether they have children," meaning some workers, particularly those on higher incomes or with significant savings, would receive less than 80% of their usual wages.

Source URL: https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/54633147

## Final Judgement
**Summary of Key Insights:**
The fact-check reveals that while the UK government did announce a combination of the Job Support Scheme (JSS) and Universal Credit (UC) for employees in Tier 3 areas unable to work, the claim that this would "mean they get 80% of their wages" was not universally true. Official guidance clarified that the 80% figure applied only to "those on low incomes getting the full entitlement" of UC. For others, the combined support could be lower (e.g., 73% in one cited example), and the JSS had a monthly cap that could further reduce support for higher earners. The eligibility for the 80% outcome depended on individual financial circumstances, not just location or employment status.

**Information Still Lacking:**
No further information is needed. The evidence clearly addresses the claim’s accuracy and its limitations.

**Conclusion:**
The claim is technically true for a specific subset of workers (low-income employees receiving full UC) but misleadingly presents this as a universal guarantee for all affected employees. By omitting the significant conditions and variations, the claim creates a different impression than the policy’s actual scope, which constitutes cherry-picking. Therefore, the decision is `cherry-picking`.

### Verdict: CHERRY_PICKING

### Justification
While the UK government announced a combination of the Job Support Scheme (JSS) and Universal Credit (UC) for employees unable to work in Tier 3 areas, the claim that this would provide 80% of wages is misleading. Official guidance clarified that the 80% figure applied **only to low-income workers receiving the full UC entitlement**, not as a universal guarantee. For many others, the support was lower due to JSS caps (e.g., a maximum of £2,100 per month) and individual UC eligibility factors like savings and family circumstances, resulting in some receiving as little as 73% of their usual income.